WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.624
The recall versus latency trade-off, measured.

2
00:00:02.904 --> 00:00:12.931
Every ANN method gives you a dial (efSearch, nprobe, the number of candidates to refine) that moves you along a curve: more work per query, higher recall.

3
00:00:13.211 --> 00:00:25.008
ANN-Benchmarks (Aumüller and colleagues, 2018) plots these curves for many libraries on standard datasets, recall on one axis and queries per second on the other.

4
00:00:25.288 --> 00:00:32.712
One of its findings is reassuring for practitioners: very different approaches reach comparable quality and speed trade-offs.

5
00:00:32.992 --> 00:00:36.896
The choice of library matters less than tuning whichever one you have.

6
00:00:37.176 --> 00:00:39.224
Here is how a working team measures it.

7
00:00:39.504 --> 00:00:40.891
Sample real queries.

8
00:00:41.171 --> 00:00:44.563
A few hundred from logs, embedded with your production model.

9
00:00:44.843 --> 00:00:48.875
Compute ground truth with exact search on the same vectors, offline.

10
00:00:49.155 --> 00:00:52.205
It’s slow, and it only runs once per corpus snapshot.

11
00:00:52.485 --> 00:00:53.616
Sweep the dial.

12
00:00:53.896 --> 00:01:03.496
For each setting, record mean recall@k against exact and the latency at the median and the 95th or 99th percentile, under realistic load.

13
00:01:03.776 --> 00:01:09.963
Pick the cheapest setting that meets a target you chose in advance, for example recall@10 of at least 0.

14
00:01:10.243 --> 00:01:14.467
95 at a p95 latency under 50 milliseconds.

15
00:01:14.747 --> 00:01:16.731
Re-measure as the corpus grows.

16
00:01:17.011 --> 00:01:26.248
A setting tuned at 1 million vectors may lose recall at 10 million, and IVF centroids trained on last year’s data may fit this year’s poorly.

17
00:01:26.528 --> 00:01:28.789
Put the recall check in a scheduled job.

18
00:01:29.069 --> 00:01:38.328
Then connect it back to lesson 2: confirm with your labeled set that end-to-end retrieval recall didn’t drop when you switched from exact search to the index.
